afirewall (20260817.4.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * NEW: local_networks.conf. A host behind a router that terminates a tunnel
    receives private traffic on its ordinary LAN interface, from subnets it is
    not on - and the anti-spoofing rules dropped it, because they subtract only
    the network the external interface sits on. List those networks here, one
    CIDR per line, and they are subtracted too. Silence keeps the previous
    behaviour exactly, so a host that does not need this needs no file.
    If you run this on a machine that is reached over a tunnel somebody else
    terminates - a LAN server behind a VPN router - check your spoof drops
    before assuming you were unaffected.

 -- Flattop5377 <Flattop5377@proton.me>  Mon, 17 Aug 2026 17:38:29 -0600

afirewall (20260817.3.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * A HOST COULD NOT TALK TO ITSELF, and this fixes it. Loopback traffic
    crosses the input and output hooks like anything else, both of which
    default to drop, and nothing accepted `lo` - so any service bound to
    127.0.0.1 or ::1 on a port no flag opens was unreachable FROM ITS OWN
    HOST. A loopback-only database, a local mail submission socket, a metrics
    endpoint on 127.0.0.1: all refused, with nothing in the configuration to
    suggest they would be. This has been true for the whole life of the
    package. If you are running it today, check what your host binds to
    loopback before assuming you were unaffected.

 -- Flattop5377 <Flattop5377@proton.me>  Mon, 17 Aug 2026 17:09:45 -0600

afirewall (20260817.2.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * UPGRADING FROM 20260817.0.0-1 OR 20260817.1.0-1 IN PLACE DID NOT WORK, and
    this fixes it. Those releases moved the fragment chain to a new hook
    priority, and `afirewall regenerate` validates the new ruleset against the
    kernel as it is - so the changed chain was checked against the copy of
    itself already loaded and refused with `Chain "FRAGMENTS" already exists
    ... with different declaration`. The host kept its old firewall, correctly
    and loudly, but the package and the running rules then disagreed with
    nothing to say so. Any host in that state is fixed by installing this and
    running `afirewall reload`.
  * The generated ruleset now replaces this package's tables in a single nft
    transaction rather than deleting them and rebuilding. That removes a window
    in which the host had no firewall at all - short on a good run, and
    permanent on a bad one, because a load that failed after the teardown left
    nothing behind. A ruleset that fails to load now leaves the previous one in
    place.
  * A forward table left behind by a host that used to forward something and no
    longer does is now removed by the next reload. Nothing short of `afirewall
    stop` removed it before.

 -- Flattop5377 <Flattop5377@proton.me>  Mon, 17 Aug 2026 16:38:01 -0600

afirewall (20260817.1.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * NEW: afirewall counters. What the sanity chains have actually stopped -
    forged sources, TCP flag combinations RFC 9293 forbids, fragments, port
    zero - printed with both address families side by side, because that is
    where the asymmetries between them show up and `nft list counters` puts
    the two a screen apart. It reads the kernel, so it needs root, and reads
    no configuration, so a host whose /etc/afirewall is gone can still be
    asked what its firewall has refused.
    A `-` means that counter is not loaded, which is NOT a zero: a host still
    running 20260816.3.0-1 shows `-` for IPv6 fragments because that release
    has no such chain.
    AND A ZERO IS NOT A CLEAN BILL OF HEALTH. It has three causes and nothing
    on the host can tell them apart: nothing of that kind arrived, the rule
    cannot be reached, or something upstream dropped it first.
  * NEW: afirewall counters --json, one object on standard output for
    something that consumes the numbers. Each record carries its own label
    and both packets and bytes; a counter that is not loaded has no record at
    all, so a consumer can tell "the rule is not there" from "the rule saw
    nothing".
  * Internal, and mentioned because it changes nothing you can see: the
    program gained a main() and stopped passing its parsed options around as
    a module global, two functions that nothing called were deleted, and the
    Python packaging metadata was retired - it built no wheel, and almost
    everything this package installs is a conffile, a plugin in another
    package's directory, data under /usr/share and a manpage, none of which a
    wheel can own. The Debian package is unaffected; it never used them.
  * The manpage is now stamped with the version and date by the release
    itself. Written by hand it was stale by construction, and it described
    add-service as writing a rules template, which it stopped doing in
    20260817.0.0.

 -- Flattop5377 <Flattop5377@proton.me>  Mon, 17 Aug 2026 16:24:09 -0600

afirewall (20260817.0.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * THREE SERVICES WERE DEAD OR HALF-DEAD AND ARE NOW FIXED. inbound.tcp2194
    and inbound.tcp8000 accepted connections they could never answer, because
    the rule admitting their reply was guarded by a flag that did not exist or
    was missing altogether; outbound.udp1514's chain was rendered and never
    reached, because nothing jumped to it. If you had any of these enabled they
    did not work. They do now.
  * IPv6 anti-spoofing ran behind connection tracking rather than ahead of it,
    so a forged source was tracked before it was dropped. IPv6 had no fragment
    handling at all - RFC 8200 removed fragmentation by ROUTERS, not by the
    sender - and the IPv4 fragment chain sat behind the kernel's own
    defragmentation, where it could never match. All three are corrected.
  * afirewall add-service raised NameError on every invocation and has never
    worked in any released version. It works.
  * NEW: afirewall enable <flag> and afirewall disable <flag>. They set one
    flag, refuse a flag nothing declares - so a misspelling is an error rather
    than a line that persists and governs nothing - and print one JSON object
    saying whether anything actually changed. A configuration manager can gate
    a single reload on that and leave a converged host quiet. --dry-run does
    everything except the write, validation included, for check runs.
    NOTHING REACHES THE KERNEL UNTIL afirewall reload.
  * NEW: forwarded services, for traffic this host routes to a namespace or
    another machine rather than terminating itself:
    afirewall add-service app --forward --tcp 8080 --to 10.99.0.2 ...
    ENABLING THE FIRST ONE IS A ONE-WAY DOOR. Until then this host has no chain
    at the forward hook and passes everything it routes; afterwards a chain
    exists there with policy drop, and anything else being forwarded - a
    container runtime's published ports above all - is refused unless it is
    declared too. A host that declares none is completely unaffected. Reaching
    a private range by address translation still needs a nat table, which this
    package does not emit.
  * Services are now records in /usr/share/afirewall/services.toml rather than
    sixty-six template files, which is what makes a service's rules, the rule
    reaching them and the rule admitting its reply come from one declaration
    instead of four hand-written places. Records in /etc/afirewall/services.toml
    are MERGED over the shipped ones by direction and name, so declaring a
    service of your own does not stop the package's list arriving with future
    upgrades. A template under /etc still overrides the service it names.

 -- Flattop5377 <Flattop5377@proton.me>  Mon, 17 Aug 2026 14:28:49 -0600

afirewall (20260816.3.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * A host came up with no firewall after a reboot and reported that it had
    started successfully. The generated ruleset was written to /run, which is
    emptied at boot, so the boot-time run rebuilt it rather than restoring it -
    and that run happens before the network is configured, where the external
    interface cannot be found by routing lookup. Nothing was generated, the
    tables were dropped anyway, and the exit status was 0. Rulesets now persist
    in /var/lib/afirewall and are restored at boot.
  * The two things this program does are named for what they do. `restore` loads
    the saved ruleset; `regenerate` rebuilds it from the configuration. Run
    `afirewall regenerate` after editing afirewall.conf. netfilter-persistent's
    `start` is an alias of `restore` and is what runs at boot - it sends only
    start, save and flush to its plugins, so that name cannot change.
    `restart`, `reload` and `force-reload` alias `regenerate`.
  * `save` writes the ruleset down without loading it, which is what the verb
    means. It used to be a synonym for restart, so asking to record the current
    state also tore the firewall down and put it back.
  * A run that finds no external interface in any address family now refuses and
    changes nothing, rather than deleting the firewall tables and exiting 0. A
    single family finding none is still only a warning: a host without IPv6 is
    not a host with a broken IPv6 firewall.

 -- Flattop5377 <Flattop5377@proton.me>  Sun, 16 Aug 2026 16:58:59 -0600

afirewall (20260816.2.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * A host with IPv6 never got an IPv6 ruleset. Interface discovery matched the
    human-readable output of `ip`, and the pattern for the IPv6 device name was
    the one for an IPv6 address - so it matched no normal interface, discovery
    returned nothing, and afirewall warned and carried on with IPv4 only.
    Discovery now uses `ip -json`, where that class of fault is not expressible.
  * DHCPv6 used the IPv4 port. The template said `udp dport 67`; DHCPv6 is 547.
  * DHCP replies are no longer matched on connection tracking state. A client
    that broadcasts or multicasts its request is answered from the server's own
    unicast address, so the reply is not the return direction of the request and
    a rule asking for an established connection never matches it. The reply then
    died on the chain policy and the lease ran out with nothing to say why.
    Both families now match the port pair.
  * Templates and lists install to /usr/share/afirewall and are replaced on
    upgrade without asking. A file of the same name under /etc/afirewall still
    takes precedence, so an override survives, but it is no longer the thing
    deciding what future upgrades may do. This takes the package from 73
    conffiles to one; the others are removed on upgrade, and any you had edited
    is kept beside them as .dpkg-bak rather than discarded.
  * The generated ruleset is written to /run/afirewall instead of the
    configuration directory. It is rebuilt on every start, so a location that
    empties on boot also makes it impossible to load one that no longer matches
    the configuration.
  * The external interface can be stated in /etc/afirewall/interfaces.conf as
    `external: <device>`, for hosts where the interface carrying the default
    route is not the one facing the untrusted network - a full-tunnel VPN, most
    obviously. Saying nothing keeps the previous behaviour of discovering it, so
    a host with one interface needs no configuration. A named device the host
    does not have stops the run rather than falling back.
  * Interface discovery asks about a documentation address (RFC 5737, RFC 3849)
    rather than a public resolver. No packet is sent either way - this is a
    routing table lookup - but a host may carry a specific route for a real
    service, and a documentation address names no service.
  * Traffic not addressed to this host is dropped, in both families, with a
    named counter. This needs no interface to be identified.

 -- Flattop5377 <Flattop5377@proton.me>  Sun, 16 Aug 2026 11:41:20 -0600

afirewall (20260816.0.0-2) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Depend on iproute2. afirewall runs `ip route get` to discover the external
    interface and address, and exits if `ip` is not executable - so on a host
    without iproute2 the package installed cleanly and then refused to do
    anything. iproute2 is priority important and present on any normal Debian
    system, which is why this was never seen; it was found by installing the
    package into a minimal container and running it.
  * No change to the source. This is a packaging fix and carries a Debian
    revision rather than an invented upstream version, which is what the
    non-native format from 20260816.0.0-1 makes possible.

 -- Flattop5377 <Flattop5377@proton.me>  Sun, 16 Aug 2026 09:31:58 -0600

afirewall (20260816.0.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The IPv6 ruleset failed to load entirely on any host with inbound.tcp8000
    enabled: the template matched on `ip saddr` inside an ip6 table, and nft
    refuses a whole table containing one unloadable rule. Such a host came up
    with no IPv6 firewall while its IPv4 ruleset loaded and looked correct.
  * New command `afirewall add-service`, which writes a rules template for a
    service the package does not ship - both address families, the config key,
    the include, the jump, and the reply path in the opposite table. It asks for
    the limit's posture and the reason for it, and refuses without them.
  * New templates: postgres, and udp51821 for a second WireGuard instance on a
    non-default port.
  * The default configuration is now also installed to
    /usr/share/afirewall/afirewall.conf, so a configuration manager can restore
    a known state at the start of a run and let each role add what it needs.
    /etc/afirewall/afirewall.conf remains the conffile and is unchanged.
  * Every rule carrying a rate or connection limit now records, beside it,
    whether that limit refuses excess or only counts it, and why. Inbound
    bitcoin changes from counting to refusing, to agree with the argument its
    own note makes and with the tor relay rules beside it.
  * ICMP: path MTU discovery is carried above the rate limit in IPv4, as it
    already was in IPv6, so a black-holed connection is not competing with
    diagnostics for a budget. Source quench is no longer accepted (RFC 6633
    deprecated it), nor are the timestamp, information and address-mask
    requests.
  * Comments describing one deployment's own network have been removed from the
    templates. The arguments they carried are unchanged; they are now made from
    the shape of the service rather than from one site's use of it.
  * The package is no longer Debian-native, so packaging fixes can be released
    without inventing an upstream version. It now declares nftables (>= 1.0.2),
    which is the oldest version measured to parse a full generated ruleset;
    0.9.8 and earlier reject it.

